/-! # Regular slices through closed Cohen-Macaulay fibre points

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 0570, lemma-slice-CM, specialized
to a globally flat morphism, retaining regularity of the immersion.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cm-closed-fibre-regular-slice).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A closed Cohen-Macaulay fibre point admits a regular immersion slice that is flat,
locally finitely presented and locally quasi-finite over the base, with singleton fibre.
Regularity is expressed on source neighbourhoods by actual quotient-spectrum charts,
allowing the empty regular list and allowing its length to vary with the point.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 0570, lemma-slice-CM.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cm-closed-fibre-regular-slice).

Proof plan:
1. Induct strongly on the finite local fibre dimension, uniformly in the source, base,
morphism and point, using depth at least dimension in the induction assertion.
Finiteness is lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-local-finite-krull-dimension.
2. In dimension zero, lem-trf-zero-dimensional-closed-fibre-open-slice supplies the
required open slice. On an affine neighbourhood its ideal is zero, generated by the
empty regular list; the affine ring is nonzero because the neighbourhood contains a point.
3. In positive dimension, lem-trf-cm-fibre-hypersurface-dimension-drop supplies a flat,
locally finitely presented hypersurface through the point, with Noetherian fibre local
ring of dimension one less and depth at least that dimension. Its defining section has
nonzerodivisor image in the maximal ideal of the original fibre local ring.
4. Apply lem-trf-flat-affine-chart-prescribed-germ to that germ. Equal germs agree on a
neighbourhood (def-weil-pbc-module-stalk); shrink to a principal affine open using
lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open. Localization preserves flatness by
lem-weil-pbc-localization-flat and lem-pc-module-flat-transitive; finite presentation
persists by adjoining an inverse variable with relation tq-1.
5. Use lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra and
lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization to identify the fibre local ring. The singleton
list of the prescribed section is regular there, including its nonzero quotient condition.
Apply lem-pur-fiber-regular-sequence-neighbourhood and shrink again: the same section is
regular on an affine neighbourhood. The hypersurface immersion is consequently regular.
The source open restriction retains flatness, local finite presentation, the closed fibre
point, and its local ring, hence all the dimension and depth bounds.
6. Apply induction to the hypersurface and compose the resulting slice with its immersion.
lem-sfb-regular-immersion-composition gives regularity without Noetherian hypotheses on
the ambient schemes. The composite to the base is the one supplied by induction.
7. The universal property of def-pc-scheme-fibre identifies the induced composite on
fibres. Its designated point maps to the original point, and its fibre remains singleton.
-/
theorem exists_regular_flat_locallyQuasiFinite_slice_of_closed_fibre_depth_eq_ringKrullDim
    {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S) [Flat f] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]
    (s : S) (x : f.fiber s) (hx : x ∈ closedPoints (f.fiber s))
    [IsNoetherianRing ((f.fiber s).presheaf.stalk x)]
    (hdepth :
      (IsLocalRing.depth (R := (f.fiber s).presheaf.stalk x)
        ((f.fiber s).presheaf.stalk x) : WithBot ℕ∞) =
      ringKrullDim ((f.fiber s).presheaf.stalk x)) :
    ∃ (Z : Scheme.{u}) (i : Z ⟶ X) (z : (i ≫ f).fiber s),
      IsImmersion i ∧ i ((i ≫ f).fiberι s z) = f.fiberι s x ∧
      Flat (i ≫ f) ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation (i ≫ f) ∧
      (i ≫ f).IsLocallyQuasiFinite ∧ (∀ z' : (i ≫ f).fiber s, z' = z) ∧
      ∀ t : Z, ∃ (U : X.Opens) (hU : IsAffineOpen U) (V : Z.Opens)
        (j : V.toScheme ⟶ U.toScheme), t ∈ V ∧ IsClosedImmersion j ∧
          V.ι ≫ i = j ≫ U.ι ∧
          ∃ (a : List Γ(X, U))
            (eV : V.toScheme ≅ Spec (.of (Γ(X, U) ⧸ Ideal.ofList a))),
            RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular Γ(X, U) a ∧
              eV.hom ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.ofList a))) =
                j ≫ hU.isoSpec.hom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
